  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Striped Saddlebags is a dragonfly species belonging to the Skimmers family, characterized by distinctive markings on its wings and body. This agile flyer is found in New England freshwater environments during the warmer months.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Wing Patches",
      "description": "Dark patches or bands on the wings, particularly visible on the hindwings, create a 'saddlebag' appearance",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Abdomen Coloration",
      "description": "Males display orange-red abdomens with darker markings, while females are more yellowish-brown",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size",
      "description": "Medium-sized dragonfly with a wingspan of approximately 2.5-3 inches",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Striped Saddlebags prefer slow-moving freshwater habitats including ponds, marshes, and lakes with emergent vegetation for perching and hunting.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Male Striped Saddlebags are highly territorial and will aggressively chase other dragonflies away from their chosen hunting grounds, performing impressive aerial maneuvers to defend their space.",
